Bound dashed stroke expansion per render.

Telegram-originated hardening, not an upstream backport. QDashStroker and
the raster engine's dashed lines limit pattern repetitions per segment or
per line only, so an untrusted SVG drawing many segments, strokes,
references or nested offscreen images could emit hundreds of millions of
dashes from a few kilobytes and exhaust memory and time. Even a few
thousand dashes of a wide round-capped pen flatten into millions of
outline vertices.

Add QDashBudget, a private scope that QtSvg arms around each untrusted
render. While the outermost budget on a thread lives, every iteration of
the dash loops in QDashStroker::processCurrentSubpath() and
QRasterPaintEnginePrivate::rasterizeLine_dashed() is charged to its step
allowance, and the geometry each dash adds downstream to its geometry
allowance: the outline vertices QOutlineMapper stores for every dash
piece QDashStroker starts (both sides, the closing line and two caps; a
round cap counts the points QBezier::addToPolygon() can make of two
quarter circles of the pen's radius at the outline mapper's flatness,
at most 512 each), and for raster dashes with square caps a unit per
256 pixels of the width * width the caps add, as each dash is filled on
its own. Neither allowance is ever overdrawn. A segment or line is
dashed only if n * (int(periods) + 3) + 1 iterations, a bound on its
worst case for n pattern entries, fit the remaining steps and the
geometry of one dash per iteration fits the remaining geometry;
otherwise the existing repetition-limit fallback draws it solid. An
integer guard ends a segment or line at that bound if rounding stalls
progress, and a subpath whose pattern or offset is not finite, or whose
odd pattern has an entry the loop never visits, is skipped. Without a
budget nothing changes.

